\chapter{Output for humans and for pipes, the
\texttt{cli.utils.output} module}%
\label{cli.utils.output}

Every listing command in nytid serves two audiences that want opposite
things from the same data.
A person running [[nytid courses ls]] in a terminal wants labeled,
aligned columns.
A shell script running the very same command wants bytes it can cut
apart; the hourly cron wrapper we ship, [[nytid.hourly.sh]], contains
loops such as
\begin{quote}
[[for course in $(nytid courses mine ls); do ... ; done]]
\end{quote}
which word-split the output and would break the instant a header row,
a box-drawing character or an ANSI colour escape appeared in it.

Four commands---[[nytid schedule show]], [[nytid todo ls]],
[[nytid track ls]] and [[nytid track today]]---already resolve that
conflict, each by branching on [[sys.stdout.isatty()]] and building a
[[rich.table.Table]] on the terminal side.
Because each of them spells out that construction itself, every
further listing that wanted the same treatment had to copy some
fifteen lines or stay plain, and the four copies were free to drift
apart.
This module reduces the whole thing to one call, [[print_rows]].
Uniformity is the real payoff: a single definition of what a nytid
listing looks like means all of them look alike on screen and behave
alike in a pipe.

The same two audiences meet again in free text.
A todo's notes are written in Markdown, and a person reading
[[nytid todo view]] wants that Markdown \emph{rendered}---headings set
off, lists bulleted, code distinguished---while an agent reading the
very same command wants the Markdown source it can quote and edit.
The second helper here, [[print_markdown]]
(\cref{sec:output-markdown}), makes that split once so every command
with notes to show can make it the same way.

\section{The three rules}

The helper commits to three rules.
The first two are the two audiences; the third is the case where
serving one of them well would mislead the other.

\begin{description}
\item[On a terminal, an aligned table with a header.]
  Rich sizes the columns to their contents, so ragged data---durations,
  labels and paths of wildly different widths---still lines up.
  We reuse the exact [[Table]] keyword arguments the four existing
  listings use, so a new listing is visually indistinguishable from
  them.

\item[In a pipe, headerless tab-separated rows.]
  The tab is the delimiter because [[cut]] and [[awk]] already default
  to it, and because it cannot occur inside the values nytid lists
  (course names, usernames, ISO timestamps, paths).
  Omitting the header is the less obvious half.
  A header line is a row that every consumer has to recognise and
  discard, and none of the shell idioms above can: [[$(...)]] word-splits
  it like data, [[wc -l]] counts it, [[sort]] sorts it into the middle.
  [[nytid schedule show]] already made this call for its CSV output;
  we make the same one everywhere.

\item[Nothing at all when there are no rows.]
  An empty listing must contribute nothing to a pipeline, so that
  [[$(...)]] yields the empty string and [[wc -l]] yields zero.
  Printing just the header would turn an empty result into what looks
  like a one-line result---and a terminal user reading a header with
  no rows under it learns nothing that silence does not already tell
  them.
\end{description}

\section{Why this lives under \texttt{cli.utils}}

A helper-only module cannot live directly in [[nytid.cli]].
The auto-loader described in \cref{climodule} imports every sibling
module of [[nytid.cli]] and calls [[cli.add_typer(module.cli)]] inside a
broad [[except]] that logs a warning; a module with no [[cli]] attribute
therefore emits
\begin{quote}
[[Trying to add nytid.cli.output yields: module ... has no attribute 'cli']]
\end{quote}
on \emph{every} single [[nytid]] invocation, including tab completion.

The loader for [[nytid.cli.utils]] (\cref{chap:utils}) skips modules
without a [[cli]] attribute instead of failing on them, which is
precisely what a package of helpers needs.
That is why [[defaults]] (\cref{cli.utils.defaults}) and [[labels]]
(\cref{cli.utils.labels}) live here, and this module follows them.

\section{Module structure}

<<[[output.py]]>>=
"""Adapt CLI output to a terminal or to a pipe.

CLI listing commands call `print_rows(columns, rows)` rather than
printing their lines themselves, so that every listing gets aligned
columns interactively and byte-stable tab-separated output when piped.
Commands that show Markdown notes call `print_markdown(text)` to get
the text rendered on a terminal and passed through verbatim in a pipe.
See the ``cli.utils.output`` chapter in the manual for the rationale.
"""
